By combining and sampling various nonlinear function combinations of diffusion processes, we determine the characteristics of resulting diffusion and stochastic equations. This approach encompasses a strategy for solving stochastic integral equations and identifies key conditions for ensuring the Markov property in the resulting processes. We further explore the statistical properties of resulting samples, including distributions and specific moments. A key application of these results is the derivation of maximum likelihood estimators for the parameters of Feller diffusions, initially introduced by Garsia and Remesnikova, for their diffusion generators. These estimators are found to be consistent and have asymptotically normal distributions relative to the probability measures governing the diffusion processes. Notably, the same results apply broadly and are not exclusive to Feller diffusions. The methods and techniques used here are versatile and suitable for a wide class of one- and multi-dimensional diffusion processes. The majority of the presented material is novel, with some existing results, initially published in the journal Random Fields and Dynamics in 2010, have been revised and expanded upon. \medskip \medskip \mathbf{Key words.} Combining and sampling, Feller diffusions, maximum likelihood estimators, robustness, spectral theory, statistical parametric models, weak convergence. \mathbf{2010 \thinspace MSCE-CLassification.} 60H10, 60F05, 62E20, 62F40. \end{abstract}

